from pathlib import Path
import re,yaml,json,shutil
r=Path('physics'); out=r/'research/physics-cm-qm-gr-79-2026-10-11-qm-composites-repair'; changed=set(json.loads((out/'changed.json').read_text()))
def read(i):
 p=r/'items'/f'{i}.md'
 if not (out/'before'/p.name).exists(): shutil.copy2(p,out/'before'/p.name)
 return p.read_text()
def deps(s,ds):
 fm=yaml.safe_load(re.split(r'^---\s*$',s,flags=re.M)[1])
 for d in ds:
  if d not in fm['deps']: s=s.replace('deps: [','deps: ['+d+', ',1).replace('dependency_roles:\n','dependency_roles:\n  '+d+': mathematical-premise\n',1)
 return s

i='lem-nrqm-composites-continuous-measurable-kraus-instrument'; s=read(i)
s=deps(s,['lem-nuclear-series-characterizes-trace-norm','thm-trace-is-absolutely-convergent-and-basis-independent','thm-cauchy-schwarz-in-an-inner-product-space'])
s=s.replace('so on simple/finite $\\psi$-combinations $W$ preserves the inner product and extends to an isometry on $H$','so the resulting equivalence class belongs to $L^2$ and has norm $\\|\\psi\\|$')
s=s.replace('## Proof',r'''[F5] Nuclear representations converge in trace norm as well as operator norm; finite rank-one sums are trace-norm dense, and $\operatorname{tr}(T_{u,v}A)=\langle Au,v\rangle$ for bounded $A$. Cauchy–Schwarz applies both to the countable coordinate sum and to the scalar integral ([[lem-nuclear-series-characterizes-trace-norm]], [[thm-trace-is-absolutely-convergent-and-basis-independent]], [[lem-nrqm-composites-rank-one-operator-calculus]], [[thm-cauchy-schwarz-in-an-inner-product-space]]).

## Proof''')
s=s[:s.index('1.1 [The isometry]')]+r'''1.1 [The $L^2$ isometry] Fix $\psi\in H$. The measurable nonnegative function $g_\psi(x):=\sum_r\|M_r(x)\psi\|^2$ has finite integral $\|\psi\|^2$, so it is finite outside a null set. On that set the finite coordinate truncations converge in $\ell^2(H)$; define the vector field to be zero on the exceptional null set. The truncations are strongly measurable and their pointwise limit is strongly measurable, as follows directly by approximation with simple functions (or by separability of $\ell^2(H)$ and measurable coordinates). The field belongs to $L^2(X;\ell^2(H))$ because its squared norm is $g_\psi$ almost everywhere. Its equivalence class defines $W\psi$, independently of the null-set representative. For any finite collection of vectors, the coordinate linear identities hold outside the union of their exceptional sets, so $W$ is linear as a map of equivalence classes; normalization gives $\|W\psi\|=\|\psi\|$, hence $W$ is a linear isometry. No $L^1$ integrability of this raw vector field is needed. [given, F1, F3, construct, algebra]

2.1 [The PVM and the effects] Multiplication by $\mathbf1_B$ on $L^2$ is a bounded self-adjoint projection, with $P(X)=I$ and $P(B\cap C)=P(B)P(C)$. For disjoint $B_j$ with union $B$, the squared $L^2$ norm of $(P(B)-\sum_{j\le N}P(B_j))f$ is the integral of $\|f(x)\|^2$ over the tail of that disjoint union, and tends to zero by scalar dominated convergence. Thus $P$ is a PVM. Compression gives positive contractions $F(B)=W^*P(B)W$, normalization $F(X)=I$, and weak countable additivity. Equivalently, for every $u,v$, $\langle F(B)u,v\rangle=\int_B\sum_r\langle M_r(x)u,M_r(x)v\rangle\,d\mu$, with absolute convergence by Cauchy–Schwarz and normalization. This is the normalized POVM asserted in (b). [step 1.1, F2, F3, F4, F5, algebra]

2.2 [Integrable cross fields] For $u,v\in H$, each $x\mapsto T_{M_r(x)u,M_r(x)v}$ is strongly measurable in trace norm by [F2]. By Cauchy–Schwarz twice and normalization,
$$\int_X\sum_r\|M_r(x)u\|\,\|M_r(x)v\|\,d\mu\le\|u\|\,\|v\|.$$
Thus, outside a null set, the rank-one series converges absolutely in trace norm. Its sum $K_{u,v}(x)$ is strongly measurable and Bochner integrable by [F3], with integrated norm at most $\|u\|\|v\|$. Define $E_B(u,v):=\int_BK_{u,v}(x)\,d\mu$. This is sesquilinear, satisfies $\|E_B(u,v)\|_1\le\|u\|\|v\|$, and has pairing $\operatorname{tr}(E_B(u,v)A)=\sum_r\int_B\langle A M_r(x)u,M_r(x)v\rangle d\mu$ for bounded $A$, by the boundedness of trace pairings and absolute integrability. In particular $E_B(u,u)$ is positive and $\operatorname{tr}E_B(u,u)=\langle F(B)u,u\rangle$. [step 1.1, step 2.1, F2, F3, F4, F5, construct, algebra]

3.1 [The bounded linear instrument] For a nuclear representation $T=\sum_jT_{u_j,v_j}$, define $I_B(T):=\sum_jE_B(u_j,v_j)$, converging in trace norm. The bound of step 2.2 gives $\|I_B(T)\|_1\le\sum_j\|u_j\|\|v_j\|$. This is independent of representation: for a nuclear representation of zero, Tonelli and step 2.2 allow exchanging its $j$ sum, the $r$ sum and each bounded-$A$ trace pairing with the integral. For each fixed $r,x$, the two-sided ideal bound makes $\sum_jM_r(x)T_{u_j,v_j}M_r(x)^*=M_r(x)0M_r(x)^*=0$ in trace norm. Therefore all the resulting pairings vanish; rank-one test operators and [F5] show the image is zero. The difference of two representations is such a zero representation, proving independence. Hence $I_B$ is linear, bounded with norm at most one by taking the nuclear infimum, and positive by positive spectral rank-one expansion. For positive $\rho=\sum_nw_nT_{u_n,u_n}$ its definition is exactly the Bochner formula in (c), and absolute integrability gives the stated bounded-$A$ pairing. Taking $A=I$ gives $\operatorname{tr}I_B(T)=\operatorname{tr}(TF(B))$ first for rank-one inputs by step 2.1 and then for all inputs by nuclear continuity; in particular $I_X$ is trace preserving. [step 2.1, step 2.2, F2, F3, F4, F5, construct, algebra]

3.2 [Countable additivity and complete positivity] For disjoint $B_j$ with union $B$, Bochner integrability gives $E_B(u,v)=\sum_jE_{B_j}(u,v)$ in trace norm. For a nuclear input, the sum of the norms of the omitted event tails is dominated term by term by $\|u_k\|\|v_k\|$; scalar dominated convergence passes countable additivity to $I_B(T)$ in trace norm. For each finite ancilla $\mathbb C^n$, act on $\mathbb C^n\widehat\otimes H$ with the pointwise Kraus maps $I\otimes M_r(x)$. For a vector $\xi=\sum_{k=1}^ne_k\otimes\xi_k$, the integrated squared output norm is $\sum_k\int_X\sum_r\|M_r(x)\xi_k\|^2d\mu=\|\xi\|^2$. The same construction as steps 2.2–3.1 therefore maps each positive rank-one $T_{\xi,\xi}$ to an integral of positive rank-one operators and extends positively to every positive trace-class amplified input. Expanding its finite ancilla matrix entries shows this extension equals the finite amplification of $I_B$. Thus every $I_B$ is completely positive, and together with additivity and trace preservation this establishes the full abstract instrument, not merely its restriction to positive inputs. [step 2.2, step 3.1, F2, F3, F4, F5, algebra]

4.1 [Conditional updates] For a density operator $\rho$, $I_B(\rho)\ge0$ and $p(B)=\operatorname{tr}I_B(\rho)=\operatorname{tr}(\rho F(B))$ by step 3.1. If $p(B)>0$, division gives a positive operator of trace one and hence the claimed density operator; if $p(B)=0$ no normalized update is defined. Nothing here claims a normalized singleton density or a universal representation theorem. [step 3.1, step 3.2, F2, algebra] ∎
'''
(r/'items'/f'{i}.md').write_text(s);changed.add(i)
i='thm-nrqm-composites-infinite-schmidt-decomposition';s=read(i)
s=deps(s,['lem-nrqm-composites-rank-one-operator-calculus','lem-nrqm-composites-bounded-tensor-maps','thm-separable-hilbert-space-has-a-countable-orthonormal-basis','lem-finite-rank-operators-are-compact','thm-norm-limit-of-compact-operators-is-compact','thm-hilbert-adjoint-properties','thm-trace-of-a-positive-operator-is-the-sum-of-its-eigenvalues','thm-trace-class-is-a-two-sided-banach-operator-ideal','thm-cauchy-schwarz-in-an-inner-product-space'])
s=s.replace('where $C:K\\to H$ is the coefficient operator determined by $\\Psi$','where $C:\\overline{K_0}\\to H_0$ is the linear coefficient operator on countable closed factor supports of $\\Psi$, defined below')
a=s.index('## Facts & Assumptions');s=s[:a]+r'''For clarity, choose finite algebraic tensors converging to $\Psi$ and let $H_0\subseteq H$, $K_0\subseteq K$ be the closed spans of their factor vectors. These supports have finite/countable orthonormal bases, and $\Psi\in H_0\widehat\otimes K_0$. The conjugate Hilbert space $\overline{K_0}$ consists of symbols $\bar z$, with $a\bar z:=\overline{\bar a z}$ and $\langle\bar z,\bar w\rangle_{\overline{K_0}}:=\langle w,z\rangle_{K_0}$. For an orthonormal basis $(f_j)$ of $K_0$, write $\Psi=\sum_jh_j\otimes f_j$ and set $C\bar f_j=h_j$, extending linearly and continuously. The positive singular values and the nonzero spectrum of $CC^*$ are independent of these supports and bases, as the reduced-state identity proved below shows.

## Facts & Assumptions

**Given:** Countable Choice, arbitrary nonzero complex Hilbert spaces $H,K$, and $\Psi\in H\widehat\otimes K$. The supports, bases and coefficient map are constructed in the proof; no countable basis of the ambient spaces is assumed.

[F1] Algebraic tensors are dense in their Hilbert completion, and orthogonal coordinates have the squared-norm sum formula. A given dense sequence in a Hilbert space yields a finite/countable orthonormal basis by Gram–Schmidt ([[lem-nrqm-composites-hilbert-tensor-construction]], [[thm-separable-hilbert-space-has-a-countable-orthonormal-basis]], [[thm-parseval-equivalences-for-a-complete-orthonormal-family]]).

[F2] Bounded finite-rank operators are compact, and an operator-norm limit of compact operators into a Hilbert space is compact under Countable Choice. Hilbert adjoints satisfy the adjoint identity and $(C^*)^*=C$; Cauchy–Schwarz bounds coordinate sums ([[lem-finite-rank-operators-are-compact]], [[thm-norm-limit-of-compact-operators-is-compact]], [[thm-hilbert-adjoint-properties]], [[thm-cauchy-schwarz-in-an-inner-product-space]]).

[F3] A compact positive self-adjoint operator has positive eigenvalues of finite multiplicity, accumulating only at zero, and its nonzero eigenspaces span the orthogonal complement of its kernel. A positive trace-class operator has trace and trace norm equal to the sum of its positive eigenvalues ([[thm-spectral-theorem-for-compact-self-adjoint-operators]], [[thm-trace-of-a-positive-operator-is-the-sum-of-its-eigenvalues]]).

[F4] Rank-one operators have trace norm $\|T_{u,v}\|_1=\|u\|\|v\|$ and trace $\langle u,v\rangle$. The partial trace is bounded in trace norm with the defining bounded-operator pairing and elementary rank-one formula. Bounded tensor maps extend with their norm and adjoint identities ([[lem-nrqm-composites-rank-one-operator-calculus]], [[lem-nrqm-composites-separable-partial-trace]], [[lem-nrqm-composites-bounded-tensor-maps]], [[thm-trace-class-is-a-two-sided-banach-operator-ideal]]).

## Proof

1.1 [Countable supports and coefficients] By density and Countable Choice choose algebraic tensors $\Psi_n\to\Psi$ with finite factor decompositions. Their countably many factor vectors span closed subspaces $H_0,K_0$ with explicit dense sequences of finite rational complex combinations; [F1] supplies finite/countable orthonormal bases. The inclusion of $H_0\odot K_0$ in $H\widehat\otimes K$ is isometric and extends to its closed completion, which contains $\Psi$. Choose the basis $(f_j)$ of $K_0$. As in the orthogonal contraction construction of [[lem-nrqm-composites-separable-partial-trace]], coefficient contractions define $h_j\in H_0$ with $\Psi=\sum_jh_j\otimes f_j$ and $\sum_j\|h_j\|^2=\|\Psi\|^2$. All sums here may be finite or empty, including $\Psi=0$. [given, F1, F4, choose, construct, algebra]

1.2 [The typed compact coefficient operator] Define $C(\sum_jz_j\bar f_j):=\sum_jz_jh_j$ first on finite sums in $\overline{K_0}$. Cauchy–Schwarz gives $\|Cz\|\le(\sum_j\|h_j\|^2)^{1/2}\|z\|$, so it extends to all of $\overline{K_0}$ by continuity and completeness. The finite truncations $C_N$ have finite rank and $\|C-C_N\|\le(\sum_{j>N}\|h_j\|^2)^{1/2}\to0$, so [F2] makes $C$ compact. The adjoint has the correctly typed formula $C^*x=\sum_j\langle x,h_j\rangle\bar f_j$ for $x\in H_0$, hence $CC^*x=\sum_j\langle x,h_j\rangle h_j$. The finite sums $\sum_{j\le N}T_{h_j,h_j}$ converge in trace norm by [F4], with trace sum $\sum_j\|h_j\|^2$; their operator-norm limit is $CC^*$, so $CC^*$ is positive trace class with $\operatorname{tr}CC^*=\|\Psi\|^2$. [step 1.1, F1, F2, F4, construct, algebra]

2.1 [Reduced-state identity] Let $\Psi_N=\sum_{j\le N}h_j\otimes f_j$. The elementary partial-trace formula gives $\operatorname{Tr}_KT_{\Psi_N,\Psi_N}=\sum_{j\le N}T_{h_j,h_j}$, regarding the latter operators on $H$ as zero on $H_0^\perp$. Rank-one continuity and boundedness of the partial trace [F4] pass to the limit and give $\operatorname{Tr}_KT_{\Psi,\Psi}=CC^*$ extended by zero. Thus the nonzero eigenvalues of $CC^*$ are intrinsic to $\Psi$, independent of all auxiliary supports and bases. [step 1.1, step 1.2, F4, algebra]

2.2 [Singular vectors in the conjugate factor] By [F3] choose orthonormal eigenvectors $v_r\in H_0$ for the positive eigenvalues $s_r^2$ of $CC^*$, counted with multiplicity; finite-dimensional eigenspace bases are supplied by [F1] and their countable selection uses the stated Countable Choice. Define $\bar u_r:=s_r^{-1}C^*v_r\in\overline{K_0}$. Then $\langle\bar u_r,\bar u_t\rangle=s_r^{-1}s_t^{-1}\langle v_r,CC^*v_t\rangle=\delta_{rt}$, so the actual vectors $u_r\in K_0$ are orthonormal too. By step 1.2 their coefficients obey $u_r=\sum_js_r^{-1}\langle h_j,v_r\rangle f_j$. The trace formula [F3] gives $\sum_rs_r^2=\operatorname{tr}CC^*=\|\Psi\|^2$, and infinitely many positive eigenvalues tend to zero. [step 1.2, step 2.1, F1, F2, F3, choose, construct, algebra]

3.1 [Reconstruction] If $g\in\ker CC^*$ then $0=\langle CC^*g,g\rangle=\|C^*g\|^2$, so all $\langle g,h_j\rangle$ vanish. Thus every $h_j$ is in the closed span of the $v_r$ by [F3], and $h_j=\sum_r\langle h_j,v_r\rangle v_r$. The orthonormal series $\sum_rs_rv_r\otimes u_r$ converges in tensor norm because $\sum_rs_r^2<\infty$. Its $f_j$ coefficient is $\sum_rs_r\langle u_r,f_j\rangle v_r=\sum_r\langle h_j,v_r\rangle v_r=h_j$ by step 2.2. The orthogonal coefficient norm formula [F1] therefore identifies its sum with $\Psi$. If $\Psi=0$, the positive spectrum and the Schmidt family are empty, giving the zero expansion. [step 1.1, step 2.2, F1, F3, algebra]

4.1 [Purity and product criterion] For $\|\Psi\|=1$, steps 2.1–3.1 show that the reduced density has eigenvalues $s_r^2$ and $\sum_rs_r^2=1$, hence $\operatorname{tr}\rho^2=\sum_rs_r^4$. The difference $(\sum_rs_r^2)^2-\sum_rs_r^4=2\sum_{r<t}s_r^2s_t^2$ vanishes exactly when there is one positive coefficient. That case makes the expansion a product vector. Conversely for a nonzero product $a\otimes b$, its coefficients are $h_j=\langle b,f_j\rangle a$, so $C$ has rank one and $CC^*$ has one positive eigenvalue; for a unit product its reduced purity is one. All ambient Hilbert spaces remain arbitrary; only the supports of this particular vector were made countable. This establishes every claimed clause. [step 1.2, step 2.1, step 2.2, step 3.1, F3, F4, algebra] ∎
'''
(r/'items'/f'{i}.md').write_text(s);changed.add(i)
(out/'changed.json').write_text(json.dumps(sorted(changed),indent=2)+'\n')
